# Slice 346 (the book :375, "внешний b получает результат"; colon-declares): `R: merge(A; B)` declares the Structure R,
# and the receiver in its tail builds it. Declared again by `R: merge(B; A)`, the place R gets the new result -- first
# reads y of the first, second reads x of the second --; declared again by `R: A B`, it holds the Structures A and B
# themselves (two fields). There is one field R after the three forms. `S: S` declares S with the word S inside, the
# name answering nothing before; the repeated `S: S` declares S with the former S inside -- no cycle, one field.
A:
    int: x 1
end: A
B:
    int: y 2
end: B
R: merge(A; B)
int: first R\y
R: merge(B; A)
int: second R\x
R: A B
int: n length(R)
S: S
S: S
int: m length(S)
